insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 1, 'Material balance rests on one accounting identity for a closed tank. Which one is it?', '["F = N Et, the withdrawal equals the oil in place times the total expansion", "F = N Eo, oil expansion supplies it all", "F = Np Et, so the vacated volume is carried by the expansion of the oil that has already been produced", "F = Np Bti, the cumulative oil valued at the initial formation volume factor of 1.20000000000000 rb/stb"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Withdrawal on the left, expansion on the right, with the whole tank doing the expanding. The expansion is quoted per stock tank barrel originally in place, so it multiplies N and never Np. Oil expansion is only part of the right side, and valuing production at the initial formation volume factor is a different error, since the withdrawal needs the formation volume factor at the pressure of the survey.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 2, 'At the last Ekene survey F is 317926.842484584 while cumulative oil is 261475.039999678 stb. What are the units of F, and why is it the larger number?', '["Stock tank barrels, as both are surface volumes", "Barrels of oil plus water at the Bw of 1.02 rb/stb", "Reservoir barrels, because that oil occupied more space downhole", "Reservoir barrels, and it exceeds the cumulative because 261475.039999678 stb of water was produced alongside the oil"]'::jsonb, 2, 'F is a reservoir volume, and the ratio between it and the cumulative oil is exactly the formation volume factor of 1.21589748101760 rb/stb at that survey. Ekene produced no water at any survey, so nothing in the gap belongs to water, and dissolved gas already sits inside the formation volume factor rather than being added on.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 3, 'Which question is material balance unable to answer?', '["How much oil was originally in the tank", "What next year''s production rate will be", "How much reservoir volume the production so far has vacated from the tank", "How much each stock tank barrel of the original oil has expanded since first oil"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Material balance is a balance sheet at a moment in time rather than a forecast. It carries no rates, no well locations and no permeability, so it cannot project a rate. Forecasting rate is what decline analysis does, and the two methods stay honest by staying separate.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 4, 'The tank model is called zero-dimensional. What does that phrase deny?', '["That the pore volume can change at all", "That fluid properties depend on pressure", "That the reservoir has measurable thickness, which is why no net pay value appears in the fixture inputs", "That any position dependence enters, so one pressure and one set of fluid properties describe the whole tank"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Zero-dimensional means no length, width or depth enters the model. Every barrel comes from the same undifferentiated pot and every psi of drawdown is felt everywhere at once. Fluid properties still change with pressure and the pore volume still changes through rock compaction, so neither of those is what the phrase rules out.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 5, 'A field has two sands separated by a continuous shale, one at 500 md and one at 3 md, and every pressure was measured in wells completed only in the upper sand. Which tank assumption fails?', '["The requirement that the reservoir shape be simple", "The requirement that one pressure represents the whole tank", "The requirement that cumulative volumes run from first oil rather than from the start of each survey period", "The requirement that fluid properties follow from pressure, since two sands at different depths carry two different oils"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A sealing shale means two pressures, and an average of them describes neither sand. Shape is irrelevant to a zero-dimensional model, which cares about pressure communication rather than geometry, and nothing in the description disturbs the accounting datum or the link between pressure and fluid properties.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 6, 'A survey records a flowing pressure by mistake instead of a stabilised shut-in pressure. Which way does the in place volume move?', '["It does not move at all", "Up, because the drawdown comes out understated", "Up by 1.84503251373536 percent, the shift a 20 psi high reading makes at the last Ekene survey", "Down, because a low pressure reading enlarges both the drawdown and the expansion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A flowing pressure sits below the true average tank pressure, so the drawdown from initial pressure is too large, the total expansion is too large, and dividing an unchanged withdrawal by a larger expansion returns too little oil. A reading that is 20 psi high does the opposite and moves the last Ekene survey up by 1.84503251373536 percent.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 7, 'A 20 psi gauge error is made once at the first Ekene survey and once at the last. Why is the first far more damaging?', '["Because the engine weights early surveys more heavily", "Because early rows carry less cumulative oil", "Because the first survey''s drawdown is only 162.261240122535 psi, so 20 psi is a large share of its signal", "Because early formation volume factors are interpolated while later ones are measured in the laboratory"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Twenty psi is 12.3258025052049 percent of the first survey''s drawdown and 1.81160776151418 percent of the last survey''s 1103.99173733300 psi. Carried through the arithmetic, the damaged first survey returns 13845816.0489208 stb while the damaged last one returns 12363180.4439902 stb. Early surveys carry little signal because little has expanded yet.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 8, 'The Ekene tank falls from 3200 psia to 2096.00826266700 psia against a bubble point of 2000 psia. What is its fluid state at the last survey?', '["Undersaturated, with 96.0082626669955 psi of margin still in hand", "Saturated, since the pressure fell by more than a third", "Two phase, because free gas always appears eventually", "Saturated, because the produced gas oil ratio has climbed above the initial 400.000000000000 scf/stb"]'::jsonb, 0, 'What matters is where the pressure lands relative to the bubble point, not how far it fell. Ekene stays above 2000 psia at every survey, so no gas has come out of solution and there is no free gas in the reservoir. The produced gas oil ratio confirms it by sitting exactly on the initial solution ratio throughout.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 9, 'Rs stays at 400.000000000000 scf/stb at every Ekene survey. What follows for the two-phase formation volume factor Bt?', '["Bt falls below Bo as the oil swells", "Bt equals Bo, since the gas term in Bt = Bo + Bg(Rsi - Rs) is zero", "Bt exceeds Bo by the free gas volume Bg times 400.000000000000 scf/stb at each survey pressure", "Bt holds at the initial 1.20000000000000 rb/stb throughout, because the dissolved gas content never changes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With Rs equal to Rsi the bracket vanishes and the two-phase factor collapses onto the oil factor, whatever Bg happens to be. The oil factor itself still grows with drawdown, from 1.20000000000000 to 1.21589748101760 rb/stb, and the two-phase factor grows with it.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 10, 'A run that needs the elapsed time between surveys meets a missing observation date, or a date no later than the previous one. What does the engine do?', '["It sorts the surveys by pressure instead", "It drops the offending row and continues", "It interpolates the date from its neighbours, since only the ordering of the pressures matters to the balance", "It refuses to march the history and raises a named error"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dates must be present and strictly increasing, and the engine fails loudly, identifying the offending timestep, rather than guessing an order. That is the right behaviour, because a survey out of sequence corrupts every cumulative that follows it without leaving any visible trace in the result.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 11, 'Dividing F by Et at the six Ekene surveys gives 12139208.1074970, 12139208.1074967, 12139208.1074967, 12139208.1074967, 12139208.1074969 and 12139208.1074968. What is that constancy evidence for?', '["That the six surveys are duplicates of one another", "That the recovery factor held steady through the history", "That the tank is behaving as one pot with no mechanism missing from the model", "That the gauge was recalibrated against the initial 3200 psia before each of the six surveys"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Six surveys, six independent divisions, one answer. The withdrawal and the expansion each grew by a factor of nearly seven across the history, so the constancy of their ratio is a real test rather than an artefact. The recovery factor certainly did not hold steady, since cumulative oil rose from 38864.2338744572 to 261475.039999678 stb.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 12, 'Survey 3 of the Ekene fixture reports cum_oil_stb of 151911.968683336. What does that number represent?', '["The oil produced since first oil on 2020-01-01", "The oil left in the tank at that date", "The oil produced during the six months ending on 2021-07-01, which is 52317.2282861540 stb", "The oil the tank is expected to deliver by 2021-07-01 under the decline model from the previous course"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every volume in a survey row is cumulative from the defined start of the history. The period volume for that step is the difference between consecutive rows, 52317.2282861540 stb, and feeding a period volume into a cumulative slot is the commonest data handling error in the subject, because the run still completes and the plot still looks plausible.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 13, 'At the last Ekene survey cumulative gas is 104590015.9998713 scf and cumulative oil is 261475.039999678 stb. What does their ratio tell you?', '["It sets the gas cap ratio m", "It equals Rsi at 400.000000000000 scf/stb, so no free gas was ever produced", "It exceeds Rsi, the expected signature of a tank down 1103.99173733300 psi from initial pressure", "It gives the free gas volume in the reservoir once multiplied by the gas formation volume factor at 2096.00826266700 psia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The produced gas oil ratio comes out at exactly the initial solution ratio, and does so at all six surveys. Every cubic foot that reached the surface was dissolved in oil when it left the reservoir, which is the fingerprint of an undersaturated tank. Once a tank crosses its bubble point that ratio climbs and keeps climbing, which makes it the cheapest bubble point check available.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 14, 'Cumulative water is 0 at all six Ekene surveys. Which pair of consequences follows directly?', '["The connate water is absent, and Swi applies to the aquifer", "The rock and water expansion term drops out, and Et reduces to Eo", "The produced water term drops out of F, and no water has been produced", "The water formation volume factor of 1.02 rb/stb goes unused, so the tank needs no water properties at all"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A zero produced water volume removes the Wp Bw term from the underground withdrawal and says nothing more than that. The connate water is still present at a saturation of 0.35, still expanding as pressure falls, and its contribution stays firmly inside the rock and connate water expansion term.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-the-tank-idea', 15, 'Ekene gave up 261475.039999678 stb from a tank of 12139208.1074968 stb while the pressure fell 1103.99173733300 psi. What does that recovery of 2.15397114609312 percent illustrate?', '["That liquids and rock are nearly incompressible", "That the tank is leaking to a neighbouring block", "That most of the tank is water, since the initial water saturation of 0.35 caps the recovery", "That the fixture history stops early, because a depletion tank needs roughly 20 percent recovery before a survey means anything"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Expanding a twelve million barrel tank by a fraction of a percent of its own volume takes an enormous pressure drop, which is exactly why a small produced volume is a sensitive measurement of a large in place volume. The stiffer the system, the more the pressure moves per barrel produced and the sharper the reading that comes back.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 1, 'In the general underground withdrawal F = Np[Bt + Bg(Rp - Rsi)] + Wp Bw, what does the middle term account for?', '["The reservoir volume of gas produced beyond the originally dissolved amount", "The shrinkage of the oil on its way to the tank", "The gas still dissolved in the produced oil, valued at the initial 400.000000000000 scf/stb", "The gas cap expansion, which enters the withdrawal whenever the gas cap ratio m is greater than zero"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Free gas is bulky, so this term dominates the withdrawal in a saturated reservoir. Gas that is still dissolved is already inside the two-phase formation volume factor, and gas cap expansion belongs on the expansion side of the balance rather than in the withdrawal.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 2, 'Why does the free gas term of F vanish at every Ekene survey?', '["Because no gas was produced at all", "Because Bg is zero above the bubble point", "Because Rp equals Rsi at 400.000000000000 scf/stb, so the bracket is zero", "Because the tank has no gas cap, and the produced gas oil ratio term is only active when m is greater than zero"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tank never crosses its bubble point, so every cubic foot produced was dissolved gas and the produced ratio sits exactly on the initial solution ratio. The bracket is zero whatever Bg happens to be. Gas certainly was produced, 104590015.9998713 scf of it by the last survey.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 3, 'Survey 2 of Ekene reports 99594.7403971816 stb of cumulative oil at a Bo of 1.20600602311013 rb/stb, with no produced water. What is F?', '["46727.8893358510 rb, the withdrawal recorded at the first survey", "120111.856789091 rb", "119513.688476618 rb, the cumulative oil valued at the initial factor of 1.20000000000000 rb/stb", "99594.7403971816 rb, since with no water and no free gas the withdrawal is just the cumulative oil"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For an undersaturated tank with no produced water the withdrawal is one multiplication, cumulative oil times that survey''s oil formation volume factor. Using the initial factor instead gives 119513.688476618 rb, and leaving the factor out altogether leaves the answer in surface units, which the balance cannot use.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 4, 'A colleague computes F with Bti rather than the Bo of each survey. On Ekene the last survey then reads 313770.047999614 rb against a true 317926.842484584 rb. Why is that error worse than its size suggests?', '["It is not, since 1.30746886688920 percent is inside most reserves tolerances", "Because it corrupts the expansion side too, since Bti also appears in the rock and water term", "Because it makes the withdrawal larger, which flatters the booked volume in the direction an auditor looks for", "Because it grows as pressure falls, so it bends the relationship between withdrawal and expansion instead of merely tilting it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The error is 0.498009379309742 percent at survey 2 and 1.30746886688920 percent at the last survey, because Bo walks steadily away from Bti as pressure falls. A constant error would tilt the answer and could be spotted, while an error that grows with depletion mimics genuine reservoir physics. Note also that it makes the withdrawal smaller, not larger.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 5, 'What is the oil expansion Eo, in words?', '["The increase in the two-phase formation volume factor, Bt minus Bti", "The oil produced per psi of drawdown", "The fraction of the tank produced so far", "The difference between the formation volume factor at the survey and the one at the bubble point of 2000 psia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Eo is a difference between two formation volume factors, so it is quoted in rb/stb and says how much bigger one stock tank barrel''s worth of the original reservoir oil has become. Its reference point is initial conditions, not the bubble point, and it says nothing about how much oil has been produced.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 6, 'Which volume does Eo have to be multiplied by to give the reservoir barrels of oil expansion?', '["Np, the cumulative production", "The movable oil volume, being N reduced by the initial water saturation of 0.35", "N, the oil originally in place", "The remaining oil in place, which at the last Ekene survey is N less the 261475.039999678 stb produced"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every stock tank barrel in the tank expands, including the barrels that will never be recovered, and it is the whole tank''s expansion that pushes production toward the wells. Multiplying by cumulative production instead computes how much the produced oil would have expanded had it stayed underground, which is a quantity nobody wants and which throws recovery factors toward absurd values.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 7, 'Dividing each Ekene Eo by its own drawdown gives 0.0000144000000000000 rb/stb/psi every time. Where does that slope come from?', '["From the rock, as Bti times cf", "From the total system compressibility of the tank", "From the pressure inversion the fixture used to build its survey pressures", "From the oil, as Bti times an oil compressibility of 0.000012 per psi"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An undersaturated oil''s formation volume factor grows linearly with drawdown at the rate Boi times co, and 1.20000000000000 times 0.000012 per psi is 0.0000144000000000000 rb/stb/psi. The linearity is a property of undersaturated oil only. Below the bubble point Bo turns around and falls as dissolved gas leaves, and the whole shape of the term changes.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 8, 'Which two physical effects does the Efw term bundle together?', '["Pore volume compaction and the expansion of the connate water", "Aquifer influx and connate water expansion", "The expansion of the oil and the expansion of the water dissolved in it", "The shrinkage of the rock grains themselves and the swelling of the clays lining the pore walls"]'::jsonb, 0, 'As fluid pressure falls the grain framework carries more of the overburden and the pore volume shrinks, while the immobile connate water expands like any other fluid. Both make room for oil to leave, both scale with the same pressure drop, and material balance bundles them into a single term. No water arrives from outside on this tank, which has no aquifer at all.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 9, 'Why is the Efw term divided by (1 - Swi), which for Ekene is 0.650000000000000?', '["To remove the connate water from the pore volume", "To convert a pore volume effect onto the smaller oil volume that has to carry it", "To correct cf for the water fraction of the pore space", "To scale the term down, since only the oil-filled fraction of the pore space can compact as pressure falls"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The compressibility group describes what happens to the whole pore volume, while the balance is written per stock tank barrel of oil, and oil occupies only the fraction 1 - Swi of that pore space. Dividing by 0.650000000000000 scales the effect up rather than down, and the whole pore space compacts, not merely the part holding oil.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 10, 'With Swi 0.35, cw 0.000003 and cf 0.000004 per psi, what is the correct compressibility group in the Efw term?', '["0.00000700000000000000", "0.00000245000000000000, being the water saturation applied to the sum of the two compressibilities", "0.00000505000000000000", "0.00000932307692307692, the value obtained once the group is divided by 1 - Swi and multiplied by Bti"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The group is Swi times cw, plus cf on its own: 0.00000105000000000000 added to 0.00000400000000000000. The version weighting both compressibilities by saturation is the classic misreading and lands 51.4851485148515 percent low, while 0.00000932307692307692 rb/stb/psi is the finished slope of the whole term rather than the group inside it.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 11, 'Why does cf enter the compressibility group unweighted while cw is weighted by Swi?', '["Because cf is the larger of the two on this tank", "Because the weighting has already been applied to cf elsewhere in the term, through the division by 1 - Swi", "Because formation compressibility is a rock property and rock properties are never scaled by a fluid saturation in a balance", "Because the whole pore volume compacts, while only part of it holds water"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Water compressibility acts only on the water, which fills the fraction Swi of the pore space, so it is weighted. The rock does not compact only under the water and leave the oil-filled pores alone, so its compressibility enters whole. The relative size of the two numbers has nothing to do with it.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 12, 'Working Ekene with Swi(cw + cf) instead of the correct grouping gives an in place volume of 15218421.8713497 stb. What is the error?', '["It overstates the tank by 25.3658536585366 percent", "It understates the tank by 51.4851485148515 percent", "It overstates the tank by 64.7435897435901 percent, which is the ratio of Efw to Eo at every survey", "It understates the tank by 20.2334630350195 percent, matching the shortfall the misreading produces in Et"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The misreading cuts the rock and water term by 51.4851485148515 percent and the total expansion by 20.2334630350195 percent, and dividing an unchanged withdrawal by a smaller expansion inflates the answer. Three million barrels of booked oil, from one pair of parentheses. Dropping the term altogether would inflate the answer by 64.7435897435901 percent instead.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 13, 'The misread grouping returns 15218421.8713496 stb at survey 3 and 15218421.8713497 stb at survey 6. Why can a good fit not catch this mistake?', '["Because six surveys are too few for a fit to mean anything", "Because a constant multiplier on the whole expansion column keeps the relationship between withdrawal and expansion perfectly straight", "Because the error affects only the withdrawal, and fit quality is judged on the expansion side of the balance", "Because the error cancels between surveys, so the fitted slope stays right while the survey values are wrong"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mistake rescales the whole expansion column by a fixed factor, so every survey agrees with every other survey on a number that is wrong by three million barrels. Straightness proves internal consistency, not correctness. The only defence is to recompute the group by hand and confirm that cf enters unweighted.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 14, 'Et = Eo + m Eg + Efw. What happens to that equation on the Ekene tank?', '["Efw drops out because no water was produced", "Eg drops out because Bg is undefined above the bubble point", "The gas cap expansion term drops out of it, because the gas cap ratio m is zero for this tank", "Nothing drops out, since all three mechanisms operate in any depleting reservoir whatever its initial conditions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene has no gas cap, so the middle term is absent and the total expansion is the sum of two numbers, 0.0158974810175951 and 0.0102925998895969 rb/stb at the last survey. The rock and connate water term is very much present, and produced water has no bearing on it at all.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-terms', 15, 'Efw is 0.0102925998895969 rb/stb of a total expansion of 0.0261900809071921 rb/stb, and that share holds at every survey. What does the constancy come from?', '["From the fixed 0.35 water saturation", "From the engine normalising the drive indices so that they sum to 1.00000000000000 at each survey", "From the pressure falling in roughly equal steps of about 180 psi between one Ekene survey and the next", "From both terms being linear in the same drawdown, with fixed slopes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Oil expansion runs at 0.0000144000000000000 rb/stb/psi and the rock and water term at 0.00000932307692307692 rb/stb/psi, so their sum runs at 0.0000237230769230768 and the ratio of one constant to another cannot move. The share works out at 39.2996108949418 percent, which is far more than most learners expect and is why the term belongs in the equation.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 1, 'In the Havlena and Odeh plot for a closed undersaturated oil tank, what quantity does the slope of the fitted line carry?', '["The total expansion Et at the last survey, in reservoir barrels per stock tank barrel", "The original oil in place N, in stock tank barrels", "The cumulative underground withdrawal F accumulated over all of the surveys, in reservoir barrels", "The pressure drop from initial conditions to the latest survey, in psi"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Plotting F on the vertical axis against Et on the horizontal turns F = N Et into y = m x with the slope m equal to N. Withdrawal and expansion are the axes themselves rather than the slope, and pressure appears nowhere on this plot.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 2, 'Why does the slope of an F against Et plot come out in stock tank barrels rather than reservoir barrels?', '["Because the regression divides every underground withdrawal by the oil formation volume factor before it fits the line", "Because reservoir barrels are converted to stock tank barrels by the engine as a final reporting step after the fit", "Because a slope is a y unit divided by an x unit, and rb divided by rb/stb leaves stb", "Because the initial survey is excluded from the fit, which removes the reservoir volume terms from the regression"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The unit check is the whole argument. Reservoir barrels divided by reservoir barrels per stock tank barrel leaves stock tank barrels, so the slope of this particular plot is an oil count. No unit conversion and no exclusion of points is involved.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 3, 'The engine reports an intercept of -6.11180439591408e-10 rb for the Ekene fit. What is the correct reading of that number?', '["The regression was forced through the origin, so the reported intercept is a placeholder with no meaning", "The fit allowed a free intercept and it landed at machine zero, so passing through the origin is a result rather than an assumption", "A small volume of withdrawal, about six tenths of a billionth of a barrel, is unaccounted for by the expansion terms in the model", "The negative sign shows a slight water influx that the closed tank model has failed to capture at early time"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine fits ordinary least squares with a free intercept and then reads the slope. Landing within a billionth of a barrel of zero is as close to zero as sixteen digit arithmetic gets, so the origin was found by the data rather than imposed on it.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 4, 'The Ekene regression reports n = 6 even though the survey table has seven rows. Why is the initial survey left out?', '["Because its pressure of 3200 psia lies above the bubble point of 2000 psia and the model is only valid below it", "Because it has no cumulative gas production recorded against it in the fixture", "Because it sits at F = 0 and Et = 0, which is true of every tank regardless of size and gives an undefined ratio", "Because the engine always discards the earliest observation to avoid pressure build up effects in the first survey"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The initial row is the origin by construction. It carries no information about how much oil is present, since every tank ever drilled starts at zero withdrawal and zero expansion, and dividing by zero expansion is undefined.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 5, 'Across the six Ekene surveys the ratio F/Et takes the values 12139208.1074970, 12139208.1074967, 12139208.1074967, 12139208.1074967, 12139208.1074969 and 12139208.1074968 stb. What does that constancy mean?', '["That the pressure surveys were taken at even intervals through the producing life of the field", "That the fitted regression has forced all six points onto a single line by construction of the least squares method", "That the plot of F against Et is a straight line through the origin, stated as arithmetic instead of as a picture", "That the tank has reached pseudo steady state, so the withdrawal and the expansion now grow at matched rates"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A line through the origin is precisely the statement that y divided by x is the same everywhere. So a flat ratio column is the straight line itself, and the surviving digit wobble is arithmetic rounding rather than physical scatter.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 6, 'You compute F/Et survey by survey on a real field and the column climbs steadily from one survey to the next. What is the standard reading?', '["The tank is receiving support that the model does not account for, most often water arriving from outside", "The oil in place is genuinely increasing as more of the reservoir is drained by the newer wells brought on stream", "The pressure gauges are drifting upward, so the recorded drawdown is progressively understated at each survey", "The rock and connate water expansion term has been left out of Et, which biases every later ratio upward"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Writing the general balance as F = N Et + We and dividing through by Et gives F/Et = N + We/Et. A ratio that rises is that error term growing, and in practice the missing support is nearly always an aquifer.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 7, 'A tank is taking in water from an aquifer and the engineer analyses it as if it were closed. Which way does the apparent oil in place err, and how does the error behave over time?', '["Too low, and the shortfall shrinks as more surveys are added to the regression", "Too high at early time only, converging on the true value once the influx settles to a steady rate", "Too low at first and then too high, crossing the true value at about the midpoint of the survey history", "Too high, and growing more wrong as the field ages and the cumulative influx accumulates"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Water is supplying volume that the model has no term for, so the model attributes that work to oil and needs more oil to explain it. Because influx accumulates while expansion is tied to a drawdown that flattens, the overstatement grows with time.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 8, 'A material balance plot comes back perfectly straight, through the origin, with R squared of 1, but the rock and water term was built with the grouping Swi(cw + cf) instead of Swi*cw + cf. What happens?', '["The R squared falls well below one, which is how the grouping error announces itself on the plot", "The points curve upward at late time in the same way an unmodelled aquifer would make them curve", "The intercept moves away from zero in proportion to the size of the grouping error", "The plot is unchanged in shape and the slope comes back 15218421.8713497 stb instead of 12139208.1074968 stb"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Scaling Efw by a constant keeps Et proportional to drawdown, so the plot stays straight, through the origin, with R squared of one. Only the slope moves, by 25.3658536585366 percent, which is why straightness tests consistency and never correctness.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 9, 'At Ekene survey 4 the fitted line predicts F = 236802.932166802 rb and the observed value is 236802.932166801 rb, a residual of about -1.7e-9 rb. What does a residual of that size indicate?', '["Rounding in the floating point arithmetic itself, since this teaching dataset carries no measurement noise", "An unusually accurate set of pressure gauges and allocation meters on the Ekene wells", "A small systematic bias in the oil formation volume factor interpolated from the PVT lab table at each survey pressure", "That the regression has overfitted the six points by using both a slope and a free intercept"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Billionths of a barrel out of hundreds of thousands, with signs that alternate from point to point, is the signature of arithmetic rounding. The fixture is built noise free on purpose so that you know exactly what perfection looks like before you meet real data.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 10, 'Fitting the Ekene line on subsets gives 12139208.1074964 stb from surveys 1 and 2, and 12139208.1074971 stb from surveys 4 to 6. What is the right conclusion to draw?', '["That the extra surveys added no information here because the data is noise free and exactly linear", "That two points are always sufficient for a material balance fit in field practice", "That the later surveys are more reliable than the earlier ones because they carry a larger pressure drop", "That the regression is insensitive to the number of points because least squares weights all observations equally"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With no noise and an exactly linear model, two points define the line and the other four add nothing. Real surveys disagree between subsets, and running the fit on the early half against the late half is the cheapest uncertainty statement available.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 11, 'Ekene''s fitted N is 12139208.1074968 stb and its cumulative production is 261475.039999678 stb. What is one percent of the fitted N as a fraction of everything the field has produced?', '["About 1 percent, since a one percent slope error scales the cumulative in the same proportion", "About 46.4 percent, because 121392.081074968 stb is nearly half of the cumulative", "About 4.6 percent, because the cumulative is roughly twenty times one percent of the tank", "About 210 percent, because the fitted oil in place is more than twice the cumulative produced to date"]'::jsonb, 1, 'One percent of the fitted oil in place is 121392.081074968 stb, and set against 261475.039999678 stb of cumulative production that is 46.4258772367404 percent. Small movements in a slope are large volumes of oil, which is why residuals and intercept get checked first.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 12, 'An engineer skips the expansion terms and plots F against pressure drop instead. On Ekene that plot is also perfectly straight, with a slope of 287.979367719385. What is that slope?', '["The oil in place expressed per psi, which becomes N once multiplied by the total drawdown of 1103.99173733300 psi", "An approximation to N that is accurate to within a few percent for undersaturated tanks above the bubble point", "The depletion drive index scaled by the total underground withdrawal at the final survey", "The tank stiffness in reservoir barrels of space created per psi of drawdown, which is not an oil volume"]'::jsonb, 3, 'That slope is 287.979367719385 reservoir barrels per psi, a stiffness. It cannot become an oil count because nothing in it knows how much a barrel of this oil expands per psi, and that knowledge is exactly what Et carries and the shortcut discarded.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 13, 'Given N = 12139208.1074968 stb, what underground withdrawal does the fitted line predict at Ekene survey 4, where Et = 0.0195072800523586 rb/stb?', '["236802.932166802 rb", "195407.593210859 rb, which is the cumulative oil production in stock tank barrels at that survey", "12139208.1074968 rb, since the slope and the predicted withdrawal share a value on this plot", "0.0195072800523586 rb, which is the expansion carried straight across without conversion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Multiplying the slope by that survey''s total expansion gives 236802.932166802 rb, which matches the observed 236802.932166801 rb. Cumulative oil in stock tank barrels is a different quantity and the slope itself is a count of oil, not a volume of withdrawal.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 14, 'Your F/Et column on a real field drifts upward across five surveys. What is the appropriate Associate response?', '["Average the five ratios and book the mean as the oil in place, flagging the spread as uncertainty", "Fit the straight line anyway and report the slope with the reduced R squared shown alongside it", "Report the drift, its direction and its size, decline to book a number through it, and pass the case on for an influx model", "Drop the surveys that deviate most from the trend and refit on the remaining points"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A drifting ratio means the closed tank model is wrong, so neither averaging nor fitting through the curve produces a defensible number. Detecting the condition and refusing to book through it is the valuable Associate contribution, and quantifying the influx belongs to a later tier.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-straight-line', 15, 'On an F against Et plot, what does it mean if a later survey plots inboard of an earlier one, closer to the origin?', '["That the reservoir has repressurised naturally between the two surveys, which is normal in a shut in period", "A data problem, because both coordinates are cumulative and cannot decrease while the field produces under falling pressure", "That the fitted slope should be recomputed with the later survey excluded as a statistical outlier", "That the tank has begun to receive water influx, which pushes points back toward the origin"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Withdrawal counts everything taken out since first oil and expansion counts everything since initial pressure, so both march outward and the plot has an arrow of time built into it. A point that moves back is a bookkeeping or measurement fault, not reservoir behaviour.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 1, 'What goes in the numerator of the depletion drive index for an oil tank?', '["The cumulative oil production converted to reservoir barrels at the current formation volume factor", "The oil expansion term Eo on its own, expressed in reservoir barrels per stock tank barrel", "The oil in place multiplied by the oil expansion term, N times Eo", "The underground withdrawal F less the produced water expressed in reservoir barrels"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every drive index numerator is one supplier''s contribution converted into reservoir barrels, so the oil expansion term has to be multiplied by the oil in place before it can be compared with a withdrawal. Withdrawal itself belongs in the denominator.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 2, 'In this engine''s oil path, what does the index reported as SDI actually measure?', '["The segregation of gas from the oil column as free gas rises toward the crest of the structure", "The expansion of a gas cap, which for an oil tank is the largest single contributor to the drive", "The rock and connate water contribution, N times Efw, with the gas cap carried separately as GDI", "The solution gas that comes out of the oil once the tank falls below its bubble point pressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gas cap has its own index in this engine, so the letters SDI carry the rock compaction and connate water term instead, and the same value is mirrored into a compressibility drive field. Acronyms differ between packages, so always find the numerator before you interpret a label.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 3, 'What are the four Ekene drive indices at the final survey?', '["DDI 1.00000000000000, SDI 0, GDI 0, WDI 0, since the tank is on pure depletion drive", "DDI 0.392996108949419, SDI 0.607003891050583, GDI 0, WDI 0", "DDI 0.607003891050583, SDI 0, GDI 0.392996108949419, WDI 0", "DDI 0.607003891050583, SDI 0.392996108949419, GDI 0, WDI 0"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Oil expansion supplied 60.7 percent of the vacated space and rock compaction with connate water supplied 39.3 percent. There is no gas cap and no aquifer, so those two indices are zero, and a pure depletion label does not mean an index of one.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 4, 'On Ekene the rock and water index equals the Efw share of total expansion, 39.2996108949418 percent, to fourteen figures. Why must the two agree?', '["Because the fixture was tuned so that the two quantities would coincide at the last survey", "Because F equals N times Et exactly on a closed tank, so the oil in place cancels out of the index", "Because both quantities are computed from the same pressure drop of 1103.99173733300 psi", "Because the regression minimises the difference between the index and the expansion share when it fits the slope"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Substituting F = N Et into N Efw divided by F cancels the oil in place and leaves Efw divided by Et. That cancellation is a property of a closed tank with nothing left over, and it fails the moment water influx makes F exceed N Et.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 5, 'Which withdrawal do drive indices apportion?', '["The gross underground withdrawal F, taken at the latest survey in the history", "The cumulative oil production converted to reservoir barrels, Np times Bo, at the latest survey", "The total expansion Et multiplied by the fitted oil in place from the straight line", "The net underground withdrawal A = F - Wp*Bw, with produced water taken off the top"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The produced water is removed from the withdrawal before the remainder is apportioned among the expansion terms and the water term. Gross withdrawal, produced oil alone and total expansion are all different quantities and none of them is the denominator.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 6, 'Why do drive indices formed on the net withdrawal sum to exactly one?', '["Because the engine normalises the four indices by their own sum before it reports them", "Because the balance is divided through by its own left hand side once the produced water has been moved across", "Because the water drive index is defined as whatever is left over after the other three have been computed", "Because the least squares fit constrains the residuals to sum to zero across the surveys"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Subtract the produced water from both sides of the general balance and the left side becomes the net withdrawal while the right side becomes the four numerators. Dividing everything by that left side makes the shares add to one as an identity, with no normalisation and no leftover term.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 7, 'A survey has a gross withdrawal of 338326.842484584 rb of which 20400 rb is produced water. If the four numerators are divided by the gross withdrawal instead of the net, what does the index sum come to?', '["It stays at one, because the produced water appears in the water drive numerator in either convention", "It falls short of one by an amount that cannot be predicted without knowing the cumulative water influx", "It exceeds one by 6.02967232815101 percent, because the denominator has been made too small", "It falls short of one by 6.02967232815101 percent, which is the produced water share of the gross withdrawal"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dividing the same numerators by F instead of A gives a sum of A divided by F, which is one minus Wp*Bw divided by F. The shortfall is fixed and computable at 20400 divided by 338326.842484584, and it does not depend on the influx at all.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 8, 'Why does the choice of denominator make no difference anywhere in the Ekene dataset?', '["Because the water drive index is zero, which removes the produced water from the calculation entirely", "Because the water formation volume factor is 1.02 rb/stb, close enough to one to be neglected", "Because cumulative water production is zero at every survey, so the gross and the net withdrawal are the same number", "Because the engine reports the indices only at the final survey, where the produced water has been fully accounted for"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fixture records no produced water at any of the six surveys, so Wp*Bw is zero and A equals F at 317926.842484584 rb. A zero water drive index is a different statement and would not by itself make the two denominators agree.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 9, 'A drive index set on a real field sums to 0.94. What should be checked first?', '["The denominator convention used, since the gross withdrawal falls short by exactly the produced water share", "The pressure survey quality, because a 6 percent closure gap points to gauge drift in the recorded pressures", "The fitted oil in place, since the slope must be about 6 percent too low to produce a gap of that size", "The PVT correlations used for the oil formation volume factor at each of the survey pressures"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The convention is free to check and it is the commonest cause. Compute the produced water share of the gross withdrawal for that survey, and if it matches the gap you have found an arithmetic fault rather than a data fault, which is the whole point of checking it first.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 10, 'The Ekene drive split reads 0.607003891050567 at the first survey and 0.607003891050583 at the last. Why does it not move?', '["Because the engine reports the same final split against every timestep in its output table", "Because both expansion terms are proportional to the same pressure drop, so their ratio cannot change", "Because the field was produced at a constant rate throughout the three years of history", "Because the tank stayed above its bubble point, which fixes the drive split for any undersaturated reservoir"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Eo runs at a constant 0.0000144 rb/stb/psi and Efw at a constant 0.00000932307692307692 rb/stb/psi, so both scale with drawdown and their ratio is fixed. Staying above the bubble point is what allows that linearity here, but it does not guarantee it on a tank whose PVT is not linear.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 11, 'Ekene creates 287.979367719385 reservoir barrels of space per psi of drawdown. How much of that comes from the rock and connate water?', '["174.804596747953 rb per psi, which is the larger of the two contributions on this tank", "113.174770971432 rb per psi, nearly two fifths of the total", "A negligible amount, since rock and connate water terms can be dropped for undersaturated oil", "287.979367719385 rb per psi, because the connate water carries the whole tank above the bubble point"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Oil expansion supplies 174.804596747953 rb per psi and the rock with the connate water supplies 113.174770971432 rb per psi. The habit of calling that term negligible is a poor one on an undersaturated tank, where oil expansion itself is modest.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 12, 'Why is the gas cap drive index zero on Ekene?', '["Because the gas cap ratio m is zero and the tank never fell below its bubble point of 2000 psia", "Because the produced gas has been reinjected, so no net gas volume left the reservoir", "Because the gas cap index is only reported for gas reservoirs and is left at zero for oil tanks", "Because the solution gas ratio stays at 400 scf/stb, which the engine treats as a gas cap of zero size"]'::jsonb, 0, 'There are two independent reasons and both hold. The fixture sets m to zero so there is no gas cap to expand, and the final pressure of 2096.00826266700 psia is still above the bubble point so no free gas has been liberated in the tank.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 13, 'What labels does the engine attach to the Ekene result?', '["Drive mechanism weak water drive with aquifer strength weak, because the water index is small but not exactly zero", "Drive mechanism depletion drive with aquifer strength weak, because the rock and connate water term counts as partial outside support", "Drive mechanism depletion drive with aquifer strength none", "Drive mechanism rock and water compressibility drive, because the Efw term supplies nearly forty percent"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The water drive index came back at zero, which is what the engine calls no aquifer, and neither a water nor a gas cap contribution passes any threshold, so the classification falls through to depletion drive. The size of the Efw share does not change that label.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 14, 'At the last Ekene survey N*Eo is 192982.830457568 rb and N*Efw is 124944.012027016 rb. What is the significance of their sum?', '["It gives 317926.842484584 rb, which is F, so the two suppliers account for the whole withdrawal", "It gives the total expansion Et once divided by the oil in place from the fitted straight line", "It gives the cumulative oil production in reservoir barrels at that survey, before produced water is added", "It gives the volume of space that remains available in the tank before the bubble point is reached"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two expansion contributions add to the underground withdrawal to the last figure, which is the balance closing in front of you. Doing that addition before dividing is the cheapest check available that the indices are about to sum to one.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-drive-indices', 15, 'Ekene produced 2.15397114609312 percent of its oil for 1103.99173733300 psi of drawdown. What does that say about depletion drive?', '["That recovery is low because every barrel is paid for in pressure with no outside source refilling the space", "That the field was produced too quickly and a lower offtake rate would have raised the recovery factor", "That the tank is unusually stiff, since a typical depletion drive field recovers most of its oil before the pressure moves", "That the remaining 97.8 percent of the oil in place is recoverable once the tank crosses its bubble point pressure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With no aquifer and no gas cap the pressure budget is the whole story, and it runs out long before the oil does. That is why pressure maintenance and displacement processes exist, and neither offtake rate nor the bubble point crossing rescues the recovery here.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 1, 'Volumetrics and material balance are called independent routes to oil in place. What makes them independent?', '["They rest on different measurements: a mapped surface and logs on one side, pressures and produced volumes on the other", "They are run by different departments, so a reviewer compares two write-ups prepared without any collaboration", "Volumetrics is used before first oil and material balance only after several years of production, so the two never see the same data", "They consume the same measurements but combine them in a different order, which cancels most of the systematic error"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Independence here is a property of the input data. The geometry route consumes a gross rock volume, a net to gross, a porosity and a saturation. The thermodynamics route consumes pressures, cumulative production and compressibilities. Neither needs anything the other produces, and both can be run on the same field in the same week.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 2, 'The Ekene hydrocarbon pore volume is 2315979.7972902344 cubic metres, there are 6.2898 stock tank barrels of container per cubic metre, and the initial oil formation volume factor is 1.2 rb/stb. What is the volumetric STOIIP?', '["17480459.6747953 stb, multiplying the pore volume by the barrel conversion and then by the formation volume factor", "14567049.7289961 stb, converting the pore volume to barrels and leaving the result at reservoir conditions", "1929983.16440853 stb, dividing the pore volume by the formation volume factor before any barrel conversion", "12139208.1074968 stb, converting the pore volume to reservoir barrels and then dividing by the formation volume factor"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The pore volume times 6.2898 gives 14567049.7289961 reservoir barrels of oil at initial conditions. Oil shrinks on the way to the tank as its dissolved gas comes out of solution, so dividing by 1.2 rb/stb converts that reservoir volume to 12139208.1074968 stock tank barrels, which is the booking the geoscience ladder published.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 3, 'Which two quantities appear in BOTH the Ekene volumetric chain and the Ekene material balance chain?', '["The gross rock volume and the net to gross, because expansion is computed per unit of net reservoir rock", "The reservoir area and the oil column height, because both routes need a tank geometry before anything else", "The initial oil formation volume factor and the initial water saturation, one in each half of both chains", "The formation and water compressibilities, which set the pore volume as well as the way that volume expands"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The initial oil formation volume factor divides the volumetric hydrocarbon pore volume and also sits inside the expansion terms as Bti. The initial water saturation sets the oil share of the pore space and also appears in the rock and connate water term through the group involving 1 minus Swi. Everything else in the two chains is disjoint, which is why the independence claim needs that footnote.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 4, 'On Ekene the volumetric booking is 12139208.107496763 stb and the material balance slope is 12139208.1074968 stb. How large is the disagreement between them?', '["About 745 stb, which rounds to zero at the precision any reserves report would use", "An absolute gap of 7.45058059692383e-8 stb, which is about 0.0118 millilitres of oil", "Exactly zero, because both figures are produced by the same function inside the engine", "About 7 stb, which works out at roughly one barrel for every million barrels in place"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The absolute gap is 7.45058059692383e-8 stb and the relative gap is 6.13761666407432e-15. One stock tank barrel is 158.987294928 litres, so that gap is 0.0118454765474796 millilitres, about a quarter of a drop. The two figures come from completely separate code paths, one reading a field fixture and one running the material balance engine.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 5, 'Why do the two routes agree to fifteen significant figures on Ekene?', '["The fixture''s pressure history was generated from the volumetric booking by inverting the material balance equation", "The engine rounds both figures to a common precision before the reconciliation tile is rendered on the panel", "Six pressure surveys is enough data for the regression to converge on the exact geological answer", "The tank is undersaturated, and undersaturated tanks reconcile far more tightly than saturated ones do"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene is engine-consistent by construction. Somebody took the geoscience STOIIP, chose a production schedule, and solved for the pressure at each date that makes the tank balance, so the pressures are a consequence of the booking. The reconciliation is a closed loop, and saying so is part of reporting it honestly.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 6, 'Given that the Ekene reconciliation is a closed loop, what does the exact agreement still establish?', '["That the mapped closure and the fluid contact used by the geoscience ladder were both picked correctly", "That six surveys are enough to book oil in place on any depletion drive reservoir of this size", "That the units, the term definitions and the regression are consistent from end to end of the chain", "That the static pressure gauges used on the field were calibrated within their stated tolerance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A closed loop verifies the method and its implementation. It shows that reservoir barrels never got added to stock tank barrels, that the rock and connate water term is grouped correctly, and that the regression recovers the constant it should. It says nothing about a map, a contact or a gauge, because the fixture was never asked those questions.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 7, 'On a producing field, what level of agreement between a volumetric booking and a material balance answer counts as a good result?', '["Within about 1 percent, since a wider spread means a method was misapplied", "Within about 50 percent, since the two methods answer questions too different to compare closely", "No agreement is expected at all, so fields quote volumetrics and drop the material balance answer", "Within about 10 to 20 percent, with the remaining gap explained rather than negotiated away"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both routes are inferences built on measurements with genuine uncertainty, and a spread of 10 to 20 percent between them is normal and publishable. The valuable work is not closing that gap but explaining it, because a gap with a physical story behind it tells a reserves committee which number to use and what would change it.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 8, 'A volumetric booking of 14000000 stb sits against a material balance answer of 12139208.1074968 stb. Which statement quotes the gap so that a reviewer cannot misread it?', '["The two methods differ by 15.3287749581786 percent, computed the standard way used across the industry", "The gap is 1860791.89250316 stb, which is 13.2913706607369 percent of the volumetric figure", "The methods agree to better than 15 percent, comfortably inside normal reconciliation practice", "The gap is around 14 percent when the two answers are compared against their own average value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The same 1860791.89250316 stb gap is 13.2913706607369 percent of the volumetric figure, 15.3287749581786 percent of the material balance figure, and 14.2375536768421 percent of their mean. All three are honest and they differ by two percentage points, so the barrels come first and the percentage carries the name of its denominator.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 9, 'The petrophysicist revises the Ekene net to gross cutoff from 0.8 to 0.7 with nothing else changed. What happens to the two answers?', '["The volumetric booking falls to 10621807.0940597 stb and the material balance slope does not move", "Both fall by 12.5 percent, because the reconciliation constrains the two routes to move together", "The volumetric booking holds and the material balance slope falls by that same 12.5 percent", "Neither moves, because a cutoff is a reporting convention rather than an input to the booking"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Net to gross enters only the geometry chain, so the volumetric booking scales with it and drops 12.5 percent to 10621807.0940597 stb. Material balance never sees a cutoff, a map or an area. Its slope still comes from pressures, cumulative production and compressibilities, so it does not move at all.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 10, 'What entitles material balance to an opinion on the Ekene tank?', '["The tank is undersaturated, so its oil formation volume factor behaves smoothly across the history", "Six surveys is above the minimum sample size the regression needs before it will return a slope", "The field has depleted 1103.99173733300 psi, which is 34.4997417916564 percent of initial pressure", "The cumulative production is large enough that withdrawal dominates every other term in the balance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The slope is F divided by Et, and Et is a small number, so an error in it is magnified in the answer. Material balance becomes trustworthy when the pressures are genuine static readings and when the field has depleted enough for the expansion terms to be large compared with their own uncertainty. A thousand psi across six surveys is a firm signal; forty psi across three would not be.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 11, 'A field''s material balance answer comes back 18 percent BELOW its volumetric booking, on good pressure data and a straight line. What is the leading explanation?', '["The pressure surveys were taken while wells were flowing, so every recorded pressure is too low", "The oil formation volume factor used in the expansion terms is too small at every pressure", "The regression has too few points to resolve a slope and has settled below the true value", "Part of the mapped volume is not in pressure communication with the wells that were surveyed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Volumetrics counts every barrel inside the mapped closure. Material balance counts only the volume that responds to withdrawal. An answer that comes in under the volumetric booking is the signature of unconnected volume, such as a sealing fault or an undrilled compartment, and that is an actionable finding rather than an arithmetic complaint.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 12, 'A field''s material balance answer keeps rising as each new pressure survey arrives, and it now exceeds the volumetric booking. What should you conclude?', '["The regression is becoming more accurate as points accumulate, so the latest value is the best one", "The tank is receiving energy or volume that the closed-tank model has not been told about", "The cumulative production figures are being double counted somewhere in the reporting chain", "Oil in place genuinely grows as pressure falls and the remaining oil expands into the pore space"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A closed tank returns the same oil in place at every survey, which is what a constant ratio of withdrawal to expansion means. An answer that drifts upward as surveys accumulate is telling you a term is missing from the model, and outside support is the usual candidate. The drift is a signal to read, not a nuisance to average away.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 13, 'At the third Ekene survey the withdrawal is 183689.695545334 rb and the total expansion is 0.0151319339712031 rb/stb. What oil in place does that single survey imply?', '["12139208.1074967 stb, dividing that survey''s withdrawal by its total expansion term", "2779.58034418239 stb, multiplying that survey''s withdrawal by its total expansion", "8.23776963986997e-8 stb, dividing that survey''s total expansion by its withdrawal", "151911.968683336 stb, since one survey can only recover the oil produced by that date"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Because the ratio of withdrawal to total expansion is the same at every Ekene survey, any single survey gives the oil in place without a regression, and this one reproduces the volumetric booking to eleven significant figures. Regression is how you average six measurements that scatter, and when they do not scatter, one point is the answer and the rest are confirmation.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 14, 'Two independent routes agree on the Ekene oil in place. Which of these does that agreement NOT establish?', '["That the arithmetic chain running from surveys to slope is internally consistent in its units", "That the tank behaved as a single pressure-communicating volume across the survey period", "That a defensible volume of reserves can be booked directly from the reconciled figure", "That the fitted straight line has an intercept indistinguishable from zero at these withdrawals"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Oil in place is the size of the prize; reserves are what can be produced at a profit under a stated development. Ekene has recovered 2.15397114609312 percent of its oil in place so far, and depletion drive alone will not lift that figure far. A reconciliation says how much is down there and nothing whatever about how much of it comes out.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-reconciliation', 15, 'Suppose the laboratory oil formation volume factor is biased high along the whole pressure path, so the initial value reads 1.25 rather than 1.20. What does the reconciliation do?', '["Nothing, because the two routes share that constant and therefore move together by the same percentage", "It opens a gap of 6.69456772433017 percent, so the check notices the problem without naming it", "It reports a smaller gap, because a shared bias pulls both answers towards each other", "It fails outright, because the engine rejects a formation volume factor that disagrees with the fixture"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The volumetric booking divides by that factor and falls 4 percent to 11653639.7831969 stb, while the material balance slope rises 2.42678501535640 percent to 12433800.5908325 stb, because the same bias enters its expansion terms differently. A gap of 780160.807635600 stb opens up. The reconciliation screens for the error without diagnosing it.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 1, 'In the Associate workflow, why does plotting the withdrawal against the total expansion come BEFORE fitting a slope?', '["Because the regression needs the axis ranges of the plot before it can scale its residuals correctly", "Because a plotted point can be moved by hand if it sits too far from the trend the others establish", "Because a regression returns a slope whatever shape the points are in, and only the plot shows curvature", "Because the intercept can be read off the plot more accurately than any least squares routine reports it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Least squares will hand back a slope and a healthy looking fit statistic for points that bend, drift or double back. Looking at the picture first is the only step in the workflow that can tell you the straight line assumption has failed before you have committed to a number.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 2, 'What does the material balance engine do with a survey history whose observation dates are missing or not strictly increasing?', '["It raises a named error and computes nothing from that history, rather than guessing at the order", "It sorts the rows into date order automatically and continues with a warning attached to the result", "It ignores the dates, since the balance depends on pressures and volumes rather than on time", "It interpolates the missing dates from the surrounding rows and flags the affected timesteps in the output"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Extracting the time steps raises a named error when a date is missing or out of order. That is the right behaviour, because a cumulative that goes backwards is not a cumulative, and any influx model that needs a time step would silently produce nonsense from a scrambled history.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 3, 'At the last Ekene survey the withdrawal is 317926.842484584 rb and the cumulative oil is 261475.039999678 stb. What does dividing one by the other check?', '["That the recovery factor is consistent with a depletion drive reservoir at this stage of its life", "That the cumulative production has been reported as a cumulative rather than as a periodic volume", "That the pressure survey and the production report share a date", "That the ratio, 1.21589748101760, is a plausible oil formation volume factor at that pressure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'On an undersaturated tank producing no water and no free gas, the withdrawal is just the cumulative oil times the oil formation volume factor, so the ratio must come back as a believable formation volume factor. At 2096.00826266700 psia it reads 1.21589748101760 rb/stb, which is exactly the value the fixture carries at that pressure.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 4, 'Which relationship must hold exactly at every survey, and therefore makes a free arithmetic check?', '["The withdrawal equals the cumulative oil multiplied by the initial oil formation volume factor", "The total expansion equals the oil expansion plus the rock and connate water expansion", "The oil expansion equals the pressure drop times the rock term", "The withdrawal equals the total expansion multiplied by the cumulative oil produced to that date"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Total expansion is defined as the sum of those two terms for an oil tank with no gas cap, so the identity holds to the last digit at every survey and costs nothing to verify. At the last Ekene survey, 0.0158974810175951 plus 0.0102925998895969 gives 0.0261900809071921 rb/stb.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 5, 'The Ekene straight line returns an intercept of -6.11180439591408e-10 rb. How should that be read?', '["As a small systematic offset in the pressure gauges, worth correcting before the slope is quoted", "As evidence that one of the six surveys is an outlier and should be dropped from the regression", "As zero for any practical purpose, against a withdrawal of 317926.842484584 rb at the last survey", "As the volume of oil that had already been produced before the first survey was ever recorded"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A tank that has produced nothing and dropped no pressure has withdrawn nothing and expanded by nothing, so the honest line passes through the origin. An intercept fourteen orders of magnitude below the withdrawals is that origin. An intercept that is a meaningful fraction of the withdrawals would say the model and the data disagree about the starting condition.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 6, 'On an oil tank, which drive index slot does the engine use for the rock and connate water expansion drive?', '["The segregation slot, which on Ekene reads 0.392996108949419 at the last survey", "The water drive slot, since connate water still pushes on oil", "The depletion slot, since rock compaction and depletion are the same physical process", "None of them, because compressibility drive is reported separately from the drive index set"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For an oil system the engine computes that index as the oil in place times the rock and water expansion, divided by the withdrawal, and stores it in the segregation slot. On Ekene it reads 0.392996108949419, which is the same quantity as the rock and water share of total expansion. It is not a gas cap, and reading it as one would misdescribe the tank.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 7, 'Why does the capstone''s withdrawal at the last Ekene survey reduce to the cumulative oil times the oil formation volume factor?', '["Because the withdrawal term is always defined that way for an oil reservoir under any drive mechanism", "Because the produced gas and produced water terms are small enough to fall inside the stated tolerance", "Because the engine drops any term below one part in ten thousand", "Because no water was produced and the producing gas ratio never rose above the initial 400 scf/stb"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene stays above its bubble point of 2000 psia at every survey, so no free gas is liberated and the produced gas ratio equals the initial solution gas ratio at every date. Cumulative water is zero throughout. Every term in the general withdrawal expression therefore vanishes except the oil term, giving 261475.039999678 times 1.21589748101760, which is 317926.842484584 rb.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 8, 'The panel tile for the rock and water share of total expansion reads 39.2996108949418 percent at the last survey. What follows immediately?', '["The water drive index at that survey must be 0.392996108949419 for the indices to close", "The depletion drive index at that same survey must be 0.607003891050583 on this tank", "The oil expansion at that survey must be 39.2996108949418 percent of the withdrawal", "The rock and water expansion must exceed the oil expansion at every earlier survey too"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With no water influx and no gas cap, total expansion is the oil term plus the rock and water term, so their shares add to exactly 1. A rock and water share of 39.2996108949418 percent leaves 0.607003891050583 for the depletion drive index, which is a free check on both numbers before you submit them.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 9, 'The capstone grades oil in place to a tolerance of 20000 stb on an answer near twelve million. What is that tolerance saying?', '["That the grader will accept the volumetric booking as well as the material balance slope", "That the engine''s own answer is uncertain at about that level on this survey history", "That method is what is being graded, since no rounding you could commit is that large", "That the answer should be reported to five significant figures and no more than that"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every tolerance in this capstone is far wider than any rounding a careful learner would introduce, and any honest route to each number lands well inside it. A field that misses is therefore reporting a method error, not a precision error, and the fix is to reread the lesson that derives it rather than to carry more decimals.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 10, 'Why must the tank explorer''s aquifer selector be left on the setting labelled None while you read the capstone numbers?', '["Because that setting matches the tank, and the alternative moves the oil in place while the fit stays healthy", "Because the alternative setting disables the survey table, so the withdrawal column cannot be read at all", "Because the alternative setting is reserved for gas reservoirs and returns an error on any oil fixture", "Because the grader records which panel settings were used and rejects a submission made with the wrong one"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene is a closed depletion drive tank with no water support, which is why its water drive index is zero and its aquifer strength reads none. Handing the regression an aquifer term it does not need gives it a freedom it will spend, and the oil in place moves while the fit statistic stays high enough to look reassuring.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 11, 'An engineer computes the rock and water term with the group Swi times the sum of the two compressibilities. What does the Ekene answer become?', '["It is unchanged, because the misgrouping cancels in the ratio", "It falls to 9963879.04308834 stb, because the expansion term has been made larger than it should be", "It becomes unfittable, because the misgrouped term makes the plotted points curve away from a line", "It rises to 15218421.8713497 stb, which is 25.3658536585368 percent above the true value"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The correct group is 0.00000505 and the misread group is 0.00000245, an error of -51.4851485148515 percent in the term. A smaller expansion at every survey means a larger slope, and the refit returns 15218421.8713497 stb with an R-squared of 1.00000000000000. Nothing in the output looks wrong.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 12, 'Your drive indices sum to 0.97 rather than 1. What should you check first?', '["The pressure survey nearest the bubble point, since that is where the terms are most sensitive", "The denominator used to apportion the withdrawal, before you go looking at the data at all", "The cumulative production figures, since a missing month would remove exactly this much drive", "The regression, since an index sum below 1 usually follows from a poorly conditioned slope"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The indices apportion net withdrawal, so produced water comes off the top before you ask what drove the rest. Dividing by the gross withdrawal instead produces a sum that looks like a closure failure and is nothing of the kind. Check the convention before spending a day on the data.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 13, 'Someone plots cumulative oil against total expansion instead of the withdrawal. What does the fit report?', '["A slope of 9963879.04308834 stb together with an R-squared of 0.999989436457542", "A slope near the true value, since the two plotted quantities differ by only a few percent", "An R-squared far below 0.95, which is what triggers the engine''s low correlation warning", "A negative intercept large enough to reveal the mistake at once"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The oil formation volume factor is above 1 at every pressure in the history, so the plotted quantity is too small at every point and the slope comes back 17.9198597235109 percent low. The fit statistic stays excellent because the mistake is close to a constant rescaling, and rescaling a straight line leaves a straight line.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 14, 'A chart is drawn with the total expansion on the vertical axis and the withdrawal on the horizontal. Its fitted slope is 8.23776963986990e-8. What is that number?', '["A conversion factor between reservoir and stock tank barrels at that pressure", "A meaningless quantity, since swapping the axes destroys the relationship the straight line encodes", "The reciprocal of the oil in place, which inverts back to the true 12139208.1074968 stb", "The regression''s residual standard error, which is what a plotting tool reports when the axes are swapped"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Swapping the axes fits expansion against withdrawal, so the slope is the reciprocal of the oil in place and the fit statistic is unchanged at 1.00000000000000. The danger is that a very small number in a cell labelled slope attracts no attention, and oil in place for any field of interest is a number in the millions of stock tank barrels.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 15, 'The Professional tier opens with the ratio of withdrawal to total expansion, survey by survey. Why is that ratio the diagnostic it starts from?', '["Because it is the only quantity in the whole analysis that can be computed without any fluid properties", "Because it holds constant for a closed tank, so a drift in it says a term is missing from the model", "Because it converges towards the volumetric booking as more surveys are added to the history", "Because it is the quantity the engine regresses, so its scatter sets the reported fit statistic"]'::jsonb, 1, 'On Ekene that ratio reads the same at every survey, and that constancy is exactly what a straight line through the origin means. A tank that is importing water does not hold it constant. The drift has a direction and a shape, and reading it before choosing a model is what separates a diagnosis from a fitting exercise.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'The tank model treats a reservoir as a single vessel. What is the central assumption behind that treatment?', '["The reservoir has no internal faults or permeability barriers anywhere within the mapped closure", "One pressure describes the whole reservoir at any date, so every barrel of oil sees the same drawdown", "The reservoir is small enough that fluid moves between any two points within a single production month", "The reservoir has uniform porosity and permeability, so its expansion is the same in every part of it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Material balance is a bookkeeping statement about a volume that shares one pressure. It never asks where the oil sits or how fast it flows. That is why it needs no map and no permeability, and also why an unconnected compartment is invisible to it.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'The Ekene bubble point is 2000 psia and the lowest surveyed pressure is 2096.00826266700 psia. What does that guarantee?', '["That the produced gas ratio rises slowly through the history instead of jumping at one survey", "That the oil formation volume factor stays below its initial value at every surveyed pressure", "That the pressure surveys are close enough together to make the expansion terms behave linearly", "That no free gas is liberated anywhere in the reservoir over the whole surveyed history"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Above the bubble point every molecule of gas stays dissolved, so the solution gas ratio holds at its initial 400 scf/stb and the total formation volume factor equals the oil formation volume factor. The oil in fact swells as pressure falls towards the bubble point, which is why the formation volume factor rises rather than falls.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'What makes a row of a material balance history a survey rather than just a data point?', '["It carries a date, a static reservoir pressure, and the cumulative volumes produced to that date", "It records the pressure at three separate wells so that an average can be formed for the tank", "It is taken at a regular interval, six months apart in the Ekene case, so the spacing is uniform", "It has been reviewed and signed by the reservoir engineer responsible for the tank at that date"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The three parts have to be matched to each other. A pressure without the cumulative that produced it, or a cumulative without the pressure it left behind, cannot enter the balance. Everything the Associate tier computes comes from that matched triple, repeated once per survey.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Why does material balance consume cumulative production rather than production rates?', '["Because rates are measured less accurately than cumulatives on most producing fields", "Because rates change daily while pressure surveys are taken only twice a year on this field", "Because the balance compares volumes that have left the tank against volume the fluids gained", "Because the engine converts any rate series into a cumulative before it computes anything"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The equation is a volume balance, not a flow calculation. What matters is how much has gone, measured at reservoir conditions, against how much the remaining fluids and the rock have expanded. Rate analysis is the decline curve subject and it answers a different question.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'On Ekene the total formation volume factor equals the oil formation volume factor at every survey. Why?', '["Because the tank has no gas cap, and a gas cap is what makes the two quantities differ", "Because the produced gas is reinjected, so the gas terms cancel out of the balance entirely", "Because the engine uses per-row fluid properties, which always report the two as one number", "Because the solution gas ratio stays at its initial 400 scf/stb at every surveyed pressure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The total formation volume factor adds to the oil term the volume of gas that has come out of solution, valued at the current gas formation volume factor. When the solution gas ratio has not fallen from its initial value, that addition is zero, and the two quantities coincide.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'The Ekene history runs from 2020-01-01 to 2023-01-01 with an initial condition and six later surveys. What does the initial row contribute to the regression?', '["It anchors the intercept, since it is the only row at which the withdrawal is known exactly", "It supplies the initial pressure and fluid properties, and sits at the origin with nothing produced", "It is dropped from the analysis entirely because a pressure of 3200 psia is above the datum", "It provides the reference date from which the engine computes every later time step in days"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At the first row nothing has been produced and nothing has expanded, so its withdrawal and its expansion are both zero and it carries no information about the slope. What it does supply is the reference state: the initial pressure of 3200 psia and the initial fluid properties every later term is measured against.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'At the second Ekene survey the cumulative oil is 99594.7403971816 stb and the oil formation volume factor is 1.20600602311013 rb/stb. What is the underground withdrawal?', '["82582.2910405870 rb, dividing the cumulative oil by the formation volume factor", "119513.688476618 rb, using the initial oil formation volume factor of 1.2 rb/stb instead", "120111.856789091 rb, multiplying the cumulative oil by the formation volume factor", "99594.7403971816 rb, since no water or free gas was produced at that date on this tank"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With no produced water and no free gas, the withdrawal is the cumulative oil valued at reservoir conditions, which is the cumulative times the current oil formation volume factor. Using the initial factor instead understates it, because the oil swelled as pressure fell towards the bubble point.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'At the fourth Ekene survey the oil formation volume factor is 1.21184099489560 rb/stb against an initial 1.2 rb/stb. What is the oil expansion term there?', '["0.0118409948955951 rb/stb, the current factor minus the initial factor", "1.00986749574633 rb/stb, the current factor over the initial one", "0.00986749574633339 rb/stb, that ratio expressed as a fractional increase in volume", "0.0142091938747200 rb/stb, the difference scaled by the initial factor of 1.2 rb/stb"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Oil expansion is the extra reservoir volume that one stock tank barrel of the original oil now occupies, so it is a difference of formation volume factors and it carries units of rb/stb. Ratios and fractional increases are dimensionless and cannot enter a volume balance.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'The Ekene rock and water expansion is 0.00000932307692307692 rb/stb per psi. At the fifth survey the pressure drop from initial is 973.754391988592 psi. What is that term?', '["0.00907838710069364 rb/stb, the per psi coefficient times the pressure drop", "0.0140220632446355 rb/stb, which is the total expansion at that survey", "110204.430295735 rb, since the term is a reservoir volume rather than a volume per barrel", "9.57436187172149e-9 rb/stb, dividing the coefficient by the pressure drop instead"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The term is linear in the pressure drop measured from initial pressure, not from the previous survey, so the coefficient multiplies the cumulative drop of 973.754391988592 psi. It stays a volume per stock tank barrel of oil in place, which is what lets it be added to the oil expansion.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'With a water saturation of 0.35, a water compressibility of 0.000003 per psi and a formation compressibility of 0.000004 per psi, what is the compressibility group in the rock and water term?', '["0.00000700000000000000, the sum of the two compressibilities before any saturation weighting", "0.00000245000000000000, the saturation applied to the sum of the two compressibilities", "0.00000505000000000000, the water compressibility weighted by saturation plus the formation value", "0.00000776923076923077, the weighted group divided by one minus the water saturation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Only the connate water expands, so its compressibility is weighted by how much of the pore space it occupies. All of the pore volume compacts, so the formation compressibility is not weighted. Applying the saturation to both gives 0.00000245, an error of -51.4851485148515 percent in the term, and a group smaller than the formation compressibility alone, which is impossible.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'At the third Ekene survey the oil expansion is 0.00918514279964078 rb/stb and the rock and water expansion is 0.00594679117156229 rb/stb. What is the total expansion?', '["0.00756596698560153 rb/stb, taking the mean of the two terms at that survey", "0.00323835162807849 rb/stb, taking the difference between the two terms at that survey", "0.0000546221261104427 rb/stb, taking the product of the two terms at that survey", "0.0151319339712031 rb/stb, the sum of the two expansion terms at that survey row"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Total expansion for an oil tank with no gas cap is the straight sum of the oil term and the rock and water term. That sum is what the slope of the straight line is measured against, and it is one of the two numbers the Associate capstone grades at the last survey.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Which set of units is correct across the Havlena-Odeh terms?', '["Withdrawal in stb, expansion in rb, slope in rb per stb, which is why the slope is a volume factor", "Withdrawal in rb, expansion in rb/stb, slope in stb, which is why the slope is oil in place", "Withdrawal in rb, expansion in psi, slope in rb per psi, which is why the slope grows with depletion", "Withdrawal in stb, expansion in rb/stb, slope in rb, which is why the slope is a reservoir volume"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reservoir barrels divided by reservoir barrels per stock tank barrel leaves stock tank barrels, which is exactly what oil in place should be. Checking the units of the slope before quoting it catches the commonest data error in the subject, which is plotting a stock tank volume where a reservoir volume belongs.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'At the last Ekene survey the total expansion is 0.0261900809071921 rb/stb against an initial oil formation volume factor of 1.2 rb/stb. Why does that comparison matter?', '["It confirms that the expansion terms have been computed in the same units as the formation volume factor", "It shows the oil has shrunk relative to its initial volume, which is why the withdrawal exceeds it", "It shows the tank has expanded by only 2.18250674226601 percent of Bti, so the slope divides by a very small number", "It sets the recovery factor, since expansion and recovery are proportional on a depletion drive tank"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Total expansion is a little over two percent of the initial formation volume factor even after eleven hundred psi of depletion. The slope is the withdrawal divided by that small quantity, so an error in the expansion terms is magnified in the oil in place, which is why a field with very little depletion cannot support a material balance answer.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'An engineer drops the rock and water term entirely, on the grounds that compressibilities of a few millionths per psi cannot matter. What does the Ekene slope become?', '["19998567.2027353 stb, which is 64.7435897435905 percent above the true slope value", "12139208.1074968 stb, unchanged, because the omitted term is genuinely negligible here", "9963879.04308834 stb, because removing a term from the expansion lowers the fitted slope", "30888876.0755117 stb, dividing the withdrawal by the rock and water term instead"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Removing that term shrinks the expansion at every survey, and a smaller denominator means a larger slope. The refit still reports an R-squared of 1.00000000000000, so nothing in the output complains. An undersaturated tank leans on rock compaction and connate water far more than intuition suggests.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'The Ekene pressure drop at the last survey is 1103.99173733300 psi. Against what is that drop measured?', '["Against the pressure at the previous survey, so the drops are summed across the history", "Against the initial pressure of 3200 psia, cumulatively from the start of the history", "Against the bubble point of 2000 psia, which is the datum for all undersaturated calculations", "Against the average of the initial and final pressures, which is the midpoint convention"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rock and water term expresses how much the pore volume and the connate water have expanded since the start, so its pressure drop is cumulative from initial pressure. Using a step-by-step drop instead would understate the term at every survey after the first.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'What did Havlena and Odeh contribute to the material balance equation?', '["A way to solve it for oil in place at a single survey without needing any fluid properties", "A statistical test that says whether a computed oil in place is significantly different from zero", "A correction that removes the rock and connate water term from undersaturated calculations", "A rearrangement of the equation into a straight line whose slope is the oil in place"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Their contribution was interpretive rather than physical. The equation was already known; grouping it as a withdrawal against an expansion turns oil in place into a slope, which means the whole history is read at once and a departure from the line becomes visible instead of being buried in a table.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Across the six Ekene surveys the ratio of withdrawal to total expansion varies by 3.48314642906189e-7 stb between its largest and smallest values. What does that tell you?', '["That the oil in place is known to seven significant figures and no further than that", "That the pressure surveys were taken at nearly equal intervals across the three year history", "That the regression has converged, since the spread is smaller than the fitting tolerance", "That the six points are on one straight line through the origin to floating point precision"]'::jsonb, 3, 'If withdrawal equals oil in place times expansion with oil in place a constant, then their ratio is that constant at every survey and on every date. A spread of three ten millionths of a barrel across twelve million is the numerical noise of the arithmetic, not a physical drift.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Plotting the withdrawal against the pressure drop instead of against the total expansion also gives a straight line on Ekene, with an R-squared of 1.00000000000000. What is its slope?', '["12139208.1074968 stb, the same oil in place, because the expansion is itself linear in pressure", "0.0000237230769230768 rb/stb per psi, which is the total expansion coefficient of the tank", "287.979367719385 rb per psi, which is not oil in place and cannot be reported as such", "1103.99173733300 rb per psi, which is the pressure drop expressed as a withdrawal rate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both expansion terms are linear in the pressure drop on this undersaturated tank, so a plot against pressure drop is just as straight. Its slope has units of reservoir barrels per psi and is not a volume of oil at all. A perfect fit statistic says nothing about whether the axes carry the right quantities.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'The Ekene regression reports an R-squared of 1.00000000000000 over six points. What is the honest reading of that number?', '["The model is correct, since a perfect fit cannot be produced by an incorrect set of terms", "The data is noise-free by construction, so the points lie on a line to numerical precision", "The six surveys are too few for the statistic to be meaningful, so it should not be quoted", "The engine has clamped the value at 1, since floating point cannot represent a perfect fit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixture''s pressures were generated by inverting the balance equation, so there is no scatter for the statistic to find. It is a genuine reading of how well six points sit on a line, and that is all it is: several badly wrong term definitions also return R-squared of 1 on this same data.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'On a real field the ratio of withdrawal to total expansion climbs steadily survey after survey. What does that pattern indicate?', '["A term is missing from the model, most often volume arriving from outside the tank", "The oil in place is genuinely increasing as more of the reservoir is brought into production", "The pressure gauges are drifting, since a physical ratio of two computed terms cannot change", "The regression should be refitted using only the most recent three surveys of the history"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A closed tank holds that ratio constant. When it drifts, the withdrawal is being supported by something the expansion terms do not describe, and imported water is the usual candidate. Reading the drift before choosing a model is the diagnostic the Professional tier is built around.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'You are handed a fitted slope of 8.23776963986990e-8 from a Havlena-Odeh plot. What is the first thing to conclude?', '["The regression failed to converge, since a slope that small is numerically indistinguishable from zero", "The axes were swapped, since oil in place is a number in the millions of stock tank barrels", "The tank is very small, so the oil in place should be reported in thousands of barrels instead", "The expansion terms were computed in psi rather than in reservoir barrels per stock tank barrel"]'::jsonb, 1, 'That number is the reciprocal of 12139208.1074968 stb, which is what the fit returns when expansion is put on the vertical axis and withdrawal on the horizontal. The fit statistic is unchanged by the swap, so magnitude is the only check that catches it.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Using only the second and fifth Ekene surveys, an engineer computes the change in withdrawal divided by the change in total expansion. What does that give?', '["12139208.1074970 stb, the same oil in place the full six survey regression returns", "The average oil in place across that interval, which is lower than the full history value", "The rate at which oil in place is being revised as the field depletes over that interval", "A number with no interpretation, because oil in place cannot be computed from two surveys"]'::jsonb, 0, 'On a straight line through the origin every secant has the same slope as the line itself, so any pair of surveys recovers the oil in place. That is the same fact the constant ratio expresses, seen from a different direction, and it is why scatter is the only thing a regression is really averaging away.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'If a single survey already gives the oil in place on Ekene, why fit a regression at all?', '["Because the engine reports an oil in place only through its regression path", "Because a regression is required before a fit statistic can be quoted in a report", "Because the intercept cannot be estimated from a single survey, and it is graded too", "Because real surveys scatter, and a fit uses every survey instead of trusting one"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene is noise-free, so one point is the answer and the other five are confirmation. On a real field each survey carries measurement error in its pressure and in its cumulative, and the line is how you combine them. The plot is also what shows you a drift that no single survey could reveal.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'The depletion drive index is defined as the oil in place times the oil expansion, divided by the withdrawal. What does it simplify to on a tank with no water influx?', '["The cumulative oil divided by the oil in place, which is the recovery factor at that survey", "The oil expansion divided by the pressure drop, since the withdrawal grows linearly with depletion", "The oil expansion divided by the total expansion, since the withdrawal is that times oil in place", "The oil formation volume factor divided by its initial value, less one, at that survey pressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Substituting the withdrawal by its balance value cancels the oil in place, so the index becomes a share of the expansion. That is a free arithmetic check, since it means the indices can be read straight off two columns of the survey table without knowing the oil in place at all.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'On Ekene the depletion drive index is the same at every survey. Why?', '["Because both expansion terms are linear in the pressure drop, so their ratio never changes", "Because the engine reports only the final index and copies it back across the earlier rows", "Because the drive mechanism was identified as depletion drive before the indices were computed", "Because the cumulative oil and the pressure drop happen to grow in proportion on this history"]'::jsonb, 0, 'On this fixture the oil expansion is proportional to the pressure drop, at 0.0000144 rb/stb per psi, and the rock and water term is proportional to it at 0.00000932307692307692 rb/stb per psi. Their shares of the total are therefore fixed, which is why the split reads the same at the first survey and at the sixth.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'The Ekene water drive index is 0.00000000000000 at every survey. What does the engine report as the drive mechanism?', '["Undetermined, because a zero index means the classifier has insufficient evidence to choose", "Combination drive, since more than one term contributes energy", "Compaction drive, since the rock and water term supplies nearly forty percent of the energy", "Depletion drive, with the aquifer strength reported as none as well"]'::jsonb, 3, 'No water arrives and there is no gas cap, so everything that pushed oil out came from fluid and rock expansion inside the tank. The classifier reads that as depletion drive and reports the aquifer strength as none, which is consistent with the straight line and with the constant ratio.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'The drive index convention divides by the withdrawal less the produced water valued at reservoir conditions. Why does that convention make no difference on Ekene?', '["Because the produced water is valued at a formation volume factor of 1.02 rb/stb here", "Because the water drive index is zero, so the water terms drop out of the apportionment", "Because no water was produced at any survey, so the two denominators are the same number", "Because the engine applies the convention only when a tank has been given an aquifer model"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cumulative water is zero at every Ekene survey, so the net and gross withdrawals coincide and the indices close either way. On a field that has produced water the two conventions differ, and only the net one reproduces published index sets and closes to 1.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'The Ekene drive indices sum to 1.00000000000000. What does that closure establish?', '["That the slope agrees with the independent volumetric booking", "That every barrel of withdrawal has been accounted for by a mechanism in the model", "That the pressure surveys are evenly spaced enough for the indices to be comparable", "That the regression has found the global minimum rather than a local one in its search"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The indices apportion the withdrawal among the mechanisms that supplied it, so a sum of 1 means nothing is unexplained and no unmodelled source is quietly being absorbed. A sum that misses is a signal to check the denominator convention first and the data second.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'A depletion drive index of 0.607003891050583 at the last Ekene survey means what, in plain terms?', '["About 61 percent of the pressure drop has occurred by that point in the history", "About 61 percent of the oil originally in place has been produced by that date", "About 61 percent of the reservoir volume withdrawn was replaced by oil expanding", "About 61 percent of the reservoir is still in pressure communication with the wells"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The indices split the withdrawal by mechanism, so this one says how much of the space left behind was filled by the oil itself swelling. The rest, 39.2996108949418 percent, came from the rock compacting and the connate water expanding. Recovery on this tank stands at only 2.15397114609312 percent.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Which of these does material balance never use?', '["The cumulative water production recorded at each survey date", "The mapped area of the reservoir and the depth of its fluid contact", "The rock and water compressibilities measured on core in the laboratory", "The oil formation volume factor at each surveyed pressure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tank model works entirely from pressures, produced volumes and fluid properties. It never asks how large the reservoir is or what shape it has, which is exactly why its answer is independent of the geometry route and why the two can be compared as a genuine check.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'A field''s volumetric booking is 10500000 stb and its material balance answer is 12139208.1074968 stb. How should the gap be reported?', '["A gap of -1639208.10749684 stb, which is -15.6115057856842 percent of the volumetric figure", "A gap of 15.6115057856842 percent, with the base left unstated as is conventional", "A gap of about 13 percent, which is the same difference measured against the mean", "No gap worth reporting, since the two answers are within the normal reconciliation band"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The barrels come first and the percentage carries the name of its denominator, because the same gap is -13.5034187813660 percent of the material balance figure and different again against the mean. Here material balance is the larger number, which points at the mapped closure or the contact rather than at the pressures.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Early in field life, which route should carry more weight, and why?', '["Material balance, because a fresh pressure survey is the most recent evidence available", "Whichever gives the lower number, since a conservative booking is always the defensible one", "Neither, because a booking cannot be made until both routes have been run and reconciled", "Volumetrics, because too little depletion has occurred yet for the expansion terms to be constrained"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The slope divides by an expansion that is still tiny early on, so the answer swings with every new survey and is not yet worth publishing. Late in life the balance has been measuring the reservoir''s actual response for years while the map has not been re-shot, and the weighting reverses.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'Two engineers each produce an oil in place figure and the two agree closely. When is that agreement NOT evidence of anything?', '["When the material balance figure rests on six surveys rather than on a longer history", "When the two figures were produced in different calendar years from different datasets", "When the volumetric figure came from a map revised twice", "When both figures were tuned towards a number the partner already expected"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Independence is a property of how numbers were produced, not of the numbers themselves. Two routes that were each nudged towards an expected answer will agree beautifully and prove nothing, which is why the provenance of both figures belongs in the reconciliation note.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Ekene has produced 261475.039999678 stb against an oil in place of 12139208.1074968 stb. What follows?', '["A recovery of 2.15397114609312 percent, with 11877733.0674972 stb still in the ground", "A recovery of 2.15397114609312 percent, which is also the reserves booking for the field", "A recovery too small to be meaningful, so the material balance answer should not be quoted", "A recovery consistent with a strong water drive at this stage of the depletion history"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Oil in place is the size of the prize and recovery to date is what has actually been lifted. Reserves are a third thing again: what can be produced at a profit under a stated development. Depletion drive alone will not take recovery on this tank very far, which is what makes the remaining volume a development conversation.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'The initial water saturation used in the Ekene volumetric chain is revised from 0.35 to 0.30. What is the new booking?', '["11205422.8684586 stb, a fall of 7.69230769230769 percent, since the water share has been reduced", "12139208.1074968 stb, unchanged, because water saturation cancels between the two chains", "13072993.3465350 stb, a rise of 7.69230769230769 percent, since more of the pore space holds oil", "14078608.2193454 stb, because the saturation enters the chain twice and compounds"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The oil share of the pore space is one minus the water saturation, so lowering the water saturation raises the hydrocarbon pore volume and the booking with it. Material balance uses the same saturation in a different place, inside the rock and water term, so its answer moves for a different reason and by a different amount.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Which reconciliation is worth more to a reserves committee?', '["A 2 percent gap that nobody on the team can account for", "A 15 percent gap reported without any interpretation attached", "A 15 percent gap with a mechanism named and a test proposed", "A 2 percent gap obtained after adjusting the contact until the routes matched"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A gap with a physical story tells the committee which number to use and what evidence would change it. An unexplained tight agreement tells them nothing they can act on, and a tight agreement produced by tuning one route towards the other has destroyed the independence that made the comparison worth running.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'An engineer computes all four terms, fits the line and reports a slope, then discovers two survey dates were transposed. What is the right response?', '["Reorder the dates and reuse the fitted slope, since the regression does not depend on date order", "Rebuild the history from step one, because data hygiene precedes every computed term", "Report the slope with a note that the dates were transposed, since only the terms matter", "Refit using only the surveys after the transposition, which are known to be in the right order"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The workflow puts assembly first because everything downstream inherits its errors. A transposition means the cumulative production no longer increases with the pressure history, so the terms themselves are suspect, not just their order. The engine refuses such a history outright for the same reason.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'At the last Ekene survey the oil expansion is 0.0158974810175951 rb/stb and the rock and water expansion is 0.0102925998895969 rb/stb. What is the total expansion the capstone grades?', '["0.0130950404535960 rb/stb, the average of the two terms at that survey", "0.0261900809071921 rb/stb, within a graded tolerance of 0.00005", "0.00560488112799820 rb/stb, the difference between the two terms", "0.0261900809071921 rb, a reservoir volume rather than a volume per barrel"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sum is the graded quantity and the units are reservoir barrels per stock tank barrel of oil in place, which is what allows the slope to come out in stock tank barrels. The tolerance is wide enough that any honest arithmetic route lands inside it.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'Three different mistakes in the term definitions each return a fit statistic of 1.00000000000000 or better than 0.9999 on Ekene. What does that establish about R-squared?', '["That it is unreliable on small samples and should be replaced by a residual standard error", "That the engine computes it before the terms are validated, so its value arrives too early", "That it should be quoted only when the fit has ten or more points behind it", "That it measures scatter about a line, and a systematic error creates no scatter"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Misgrouping the compressibilities, dropping the rock and water term and plotting cumulative oil instead of withdrawal are all smooth distortions that leave a straight line straight while moving its slope. The statistic is doing its job; it just answers a narrower question than people read into it.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'A field has three pressure surveys and 40 psi of total depletion. Its material balance slope is 8 percent below the volumetric booking. What should the report say?', '["That the material balance answer is provisional, because the expansion terms are still tiny", "That the volumetric booking should be revised down to match the material balance answer", "That the two routes agree within normal tolerance and the booking is supported", "That an aquifer should be added to the model, since the answer came in low against the map"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Forty psi barely moves the expansion terms, so the slope divides by a very small and very uncertain number and will swing with the next survey. Quoting an 8 percent agreement from that data implies a precision the method does not yet have, and saying the method is not ready is the honest response.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'A colleague''s tank report shows an oil in place 18 percent below the volumetric booking, an excellent fit statistic, and a note saying the plot used the cumulative oil column. What is the diagnosis?', '["A units error, since the plotted quantity is a surface volume where a reservoir volume belongs", "An unconnected compartment, which is the standard reading of a low material balance answer", "A pressure datum error, since flowing pressures would depress the answer by about this much", "Too few surveys, since three or four points cannot resolve a slope to better than 20 percent"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The oil formation volume factor is above 1 at every pressure in the history, so plotting the cumulative rather than the withdrawal makes every plotted point too small and drags the slope down by about that much. Reading a bookkeeping error as a geological finding is how an invented compartment gets drilled.'),
  ('mbal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'The last step of the Associate workflow is to state what would change the answer. Why is that step part of the workflow rather than an optional extra?', '["Because the grading form requires a sensitivity statement alongside the six numbers", "Because a slope alone does not tell a reader how much depletion or data stands behind it", "Because reserves regulations require an uncertainty range on every booked volume", "Because the engine emits warnings that have to be reproduced in any report of its output"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two slopes that look identical can rest on eleven hundred psi of depletion across six good surveys or on forty psi across three doubtful ones. Naming the exposures, usually the representativeness of the static pressures and the amount of depletion behind the fit, is what turns a number into something a reader can weigh.');
